{"text": " How does a car actually rotate when reversing? Its rear wheels are fixed parallel to the sides of the car. That means the imaginary line passing through both rear wheel centers must always point directly at the center of the turning circle, labeled X. What about the front wheels? Because the inside front wheel travels along a tighter circle than the outside wheel, it has to steer at a sharper angle to avoid dragging sideways. This is called Ackerman Steering. Patented back in 1818 for horse-drawn carriages, when all wheel axle lines meet at a single point X, every part of the car turns cleanly around X.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.78, "text": " How does a car actually rotate when reversing?", "tokens": [50363, 1374, 857, 257, 1097, 1682, 23064, 618, 37556, 30, 50527], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06850741086182771, "compression_ratio": 1.6897810218978102, "no_speech_prob": 0.00013322211452759802, "words": [{"word": " How", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.34, "probability": 0.762319028377533}, {"word": " does", "start": 0.34, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9960687160491943}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.64, "probability": 0.9835497140884399}, {"word": " car", "start": 0.64, "end": 0.88, "probability": 0.997094988822937}, {"word": " actually", "start": 0.88, "end": 1.52, "probability": 0.9916648268699646}, {"word": " rotate", "start": 1.52, "end": 2.1, "probability": 0.9957808256149292}, {"word": " when", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.34, "probability": 0.9955742359161377}, {"word": " reversing?", "start": 2.34, "end": 2.78, "probability": 0.9961373209953308}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 3.44, "end": 6.54, "text": " Its rear wheels are fixed parallel to the sides of the car.", "tokens": [50527, 6363, 8286, 13666, 389, 5969, 10730, 284, 262, 5389, 286, 262, 1097, 13, 50707], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06850741086182771, "compression_ratio": 1.6897810218978102, "no_speech_prob": 0.00013322211452759802, "words": 